>
  <p>
    Alopecia areata causes sudden, well-defined round or oval patches of hair loss, most often on
    the scalp but possible in the beard, eyebrows, eyelashes or anywhere else hair grows. The skin
    in the patch looks normal — not scaly, not scarred, not inflamed.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is autoimmune. The immune system attacks the hair follicle and switches it off. Crucially,
    it does not destroy it, which is why regrowth remains possible even in patches that have been
    present for a long time.
  </p>

  <h2>How much it affects</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Patchy alopecia areata</strong> — one or several discrete patches. The common form, and the one most likely to regrow spontaneously</li>
    <li><strong>Alopecia totalis</strong> — loss of all scalp hair</li>
    <li><strong>Alopecia universalis</strong> — loss of all body hair</li>
    <li><strong>Ophiasis pattern</strong> — a band along the lower back and sides of the scalp; tends to respond less readily</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it is assessed</h2>

  <p>
    Trichoscopy is the key examination. It shows the characteristic features — exclamation-mark
    hairs, yellow dots, black dots — and confirms that follicle openings are preserved, which
    distinguishes this from scarring alopecia where they are lost. That distinction determines
    whether regrowth is possible at all.
  </p>

  <p>
    A KOH mount or fungal culture is worth doing in children, where tinea capitis is the main
    alternative. Because it is autoimmune, thyroid function is checked, and other autoimmune
    conditions are asked about. Nails are examined — fine pitting is common and supports the
    diagnosis.
  </p>

  <h2>Treatment</h2>

  <h3>Limited patches</h3>
  <p>
    Intralesional steroid injections into the patch are the standard and most effective approach,
    repeated every four to six weeks. Potent topical steroids and topical minoxidil are used
    alongside, particularly in children where injections are not appropriate.
  </p>

  <h3>More extensive disease</h3>
  <p>
    Topical immunotherapy, oral immunosuppressants, or JAK inhibitors depending on severity,
    duration and what has already been tried. Each needs screening and monitoring.
  </p>

  <h3>Where watchful waiting is reasonable</h3>
  <p>
    A single small patch in an otherwise well person often regrows by itself. Choosing to observe
    for a few months, with a review date, is a legitimate plan rather than a failure to treat —
    and it will be offered as an option rather than treatment being pushed.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>The part that is usually left out</h3>
    <p>
      Sudden visible hair loss is distressing out of proportion to its medical seriousness, and
      that is worth saying rather than brushing past. The condition causes no physical harm and no
      illness. That does not make the experience of it trivial, and the consultation should have
      room for both facts.
    </p>
  </div>
